==============================================================================
Win_config Functions                                          *api-win_config*

nvim_open_win({buf}, {enter}, {config})                      *nvim_open_win()*
    Opens a new split window, floating window, or external window.
    ‚Ä¢ Specify `relative` to create a floating window. Floats are drawn over
      the split layout, relative to a position in some other window. See
      |api-floatwin|.
      ‚Ä¢ Floats must specify `width` and `height`.
    ‚Ä¢ Specify `external` to create an external window. External windows are
      displayed as separate top-level windows managed by the |ui-multigrid| UI
      (not Nvim).
    ‚Ä¢ If `relative` and `external` are omitted, a normal "split" window is
      created.
      ‚Ä¢ The `win` key decides which window to split. If nil or 0, the split
        will be adjacent to the current window. If -1, a top-level split will
        be created.
      ‚Ä¢ Use `vertical` and `split` to control split direction. For `vertical`,
        the exact direction is determined by 'splitright' and 'splitbelow'.
      ‚Ä¢ Split windows cannot have `bufpos`, `row`, `col`, `border`, `title`,
        `footer`.

    With relative=editor (row=0,col=0) refers to the top-left corner of the
    screen-grid and (row=Lines-1,col=Columns-1) refers to the bottom-right
    corner. Fractional values are allowed, but the builtin implementation
    (used by non-multigrid UIs) will always round down to nearest integer.

    Out-of-bounds values, and configurations that make the float not fit
    inside the main editor, are allowed. The builtin implementation truncates
    values so floats are fully within the main screen grid. External GUIs
    could let floats hover outside of the main window like a tooltip, but this
    should not be used to specify arbitrary WM screen positions.

    Examples: >lua
        -- Window-relative float with 'statusline' enabled:
        local w1 = vim.api.nvim_open_win(0, false,
          {relative='win', row=3, col=3, width=40, height=4})
        vim.wo[w1].statusline = vim.o.statusline

        -- Buffer-relative float (travels as buffer is scrolled):
        vim.api.nvim_open_win(0, false,
          {relative='win', width=40, height=4, bufpos={100,10}})

        -- Vertical split left of the current window:
        vim.api.nvim_open_win(0, false, { split = 'left', win = 0, })
<

    Attributes: ~
        not allowed when |textlock| is active
        Since: 0.4.0

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {buf}     (`integer`) Buffer to display, or 0 for current buffer
      ‚Ä¢ {enter}   (`boolean`) Enter the window (make it the current window)
      ‚Ä¢ {config}  (`vim.api.keyset.win_config`) Map defining the window
                  configuration. Keys:
                  ‚Ä¢ anchor: Decides which corner of the float to place at
                    (row,col):
                    ‚Ä¢ "NW" northwest (default)
                    ‚Ä¢ "NE" northeast
                    ‚Ä¢ "SW" southwest
                    ‚Ä¢ "SE" southeast
                  ‚Ä¢ border: (`string|string[]`, default: 'winborder' option)
                    Window border. The string form accepts the same values as
                    the 'winborder' option. The array form must have a length
                    of eight or any divisor of eight, specifying the chars
                    that form the border in a clockwise fashion starting from
                    the top-left corner. For example, the double-box style can
                    be specified as: >
                    [ "‚ïî", "‚ïê" ,"‚ïó", "‚ïë", "‚ïù", "‚ïê", "‚ïö", "‚ïë" ].
<
                    If fewer than eight chars are given, they will be
                    repeated. An ASCII border could be specified as: >
                    [ "/", "-", \"\\\\\", "|" ],
<
                    Or one char for all sides: >
                    [ "x" ].
<
                    Empty string can be used to hide a specific border. This
                    example will show only vertical borders, not horizontal: >
                    [ "", "", "", ">", "", "", "", "<" ]
<
                    By default, |hl-FloatBorder| highlight is used, which
                    links to |hl-WinSeparator| when not defined. Each border
                    side can specify an optional highlight: >
                    [ ["+", "MyCorner"], ["x", "MyBorder"] ].
<
                  ‚Ä¢ bufpos: Places float relative to buffer text (only when
                    relative="win"). Takes a tuple of zero-indexed
                    `[line, column]`. `row` and `col` if given are applied
                    relative to this position, else they default to:
                    ‚Ä¢ `row=1` and `col=0` if `anchor` is "NW" or "NE"
                    ‚Ä¢ `row=0` and `col=0` if `anchor` is "SW" or "SE" (thus
                      like a tooltip near the buffer text).
                  ‚Ä¢ col: Column position in units of screen cell width, may be
                    fractional.
                  ‚Ä¢ external: GUI should display the window as an external
                    top-level window. Currently accepts no other positioning
                    configuration together with this.
                  ‚Ä¢ fixed: If true when anchor is NW or SW, the float window
                    would be kept fixed even if the window would be truncated.
                  ‚Ä¢ focusable: (default: true) Enable focus by user actions
                    (wincmds, mouse events). Non-|focusable| windows can be
                    entered by |nvim_set_current_win()|, or by mouse events if
                    `mouse=true`.
                  ‚Ä¢ footer: (`string|string[]?`) Footer in window border:
                    string or list of `[text, highlight]` tuples. Default
                    highlight group is `FloatFooter`.
                  ‚Ä¢ footer_pos: (default: "left") Footer position. One of:
                    "left", "center", "right". The `footer` field must be set
                    also.
                  ‚Ä¢ height: Window height (in character cells). Minimum of 1.
                  ‚Ä¢ hide: Hides the floating window. |window-hidden|
                  ‚Ä¢ mouse: (default: `focusable` value) If true, mouse events
                    interact with the window normally; if false, they pass
                    through to the window behind it.
                  ‚Ä¢ noautocmd: Block all autocommands for the duration of the
                    call. Cannot be changed by |nvim_win_set_config()|.
                  ‚Ä¢ relative: Sets the window layout to "floating", placed at
                    (row,col) coordinates relative to:
                    ‚Ä¢ "cursor" Cursor position in current window.
                    ‚Ä¢ "editor" The global editor grid.
                    ‚Ä¢ "laststatus" 'laststatus' if present, or last row.
                    ‚Ä¢ "mouse" Mouse position.
                    ‚Ä¢ "tabline" Tabline if present, or first row.
                    ‚Ä¢ "win" Window given by the `win` field, or current
                      window.
                  ‚Ä¢ row: Row position in units of "screen cell height", may be
                    fractional.
                  ‚Ä¢ split: Split direction: "left", "right", "above", "below".
                  ‚Ä¢ style: (optional) Configure the appearance of the window:
                    ‚Ä¢ "" No special style.
                    ‚Ä¢ "minimal" Nvim will display the window with many UI
                      options disabled. This is useful when displaying a
                      temporary float where the text should not be edited.
                      Disables 'number', 'relativenumber', 'cursorline',
                      'cursorcolumn', 'foldcolumn', 'spell' and 'list'
                      options. 'signcolumn' is changed to `auto` and
                      'colorcolumn' is cleared. 'statuscolumn' is changed to
                      empty. The end-of-buffer region is hidden by setting
                      `eob` flag of 'fillchars' to a space char, and clearing
                      the |hl-EndOfBuffer| region in 'winhighlight'.
                  ‚Ä¢ title: (optional) Title in window border, string or list.
                    List should consist of `[text, highlight]` tuples. If
                    string, or a tuple lacks a highlight, the default
                    highlight group is `FloatTitle`.
                  ‚Ä¢ title_pos: (default: "left") Title position. One of:
                    "left", "center", "right". The `title` field must be set
                    also.
                  ‚Ä¢ vertical: Split vertically |:vertical|.
                  ‚Ä¢ width: Window width (in character cells). Minimum of 1.
                  ‚Ä¢ win: |window-ID| target window. Can be in a different tab
                    page. Determines the window to split (negative values act
                    like |:topleft|, |:botright|), the relative window for a
                    `relative="win"` float, or just the target tab page
                    (inferred from the window) for others.
                  ‚Ä¢ zindex: (positive integer, default: 50) Stacking order.
                    Floats with higher `zindex` overlay floats with lower
                    indices. Below 100 is recommended, unless there is a good
                    reason to overshadow builtin elements. The cursor is
                    dimmed if an unfocused float above the cursor exceeds the
                    zindex of the current window by 50. These screen elements
                    have hard-coded z-indices:
                    ‚Ä¢ 100: |ins-completion-menu| popupmenu
                    ‚Ä¢ 200: message scrollback (|pager|)
                    ‚Ä¢ 250: |cmdline-completion| popupmenu (wildoptions=pum)
                  ‚Ä¢ _cmdline_offset: (EXPERIMENTAL) When provided, anchor the
                    |cmdline-completion| popupmenu to this window, with an
                    offset in screen cell width.

    Return: ~
        (`integer`) |window-ID|, or 0 on error

nvim_win_get_config({win})                             *nvim_win_get_config()*
    Gets window config as a dict which can be passed to |nvim_open_win()| as
    the `config` parameter.

    For non-floating windows, `relative` is empty, thus you can check that
    field to detect if a window is a floatwin: >lua
        vim.print(vim.api.nvim_win_get_config(0).relative == '' and 'non-float' or 'float')
        -- Or use win_gettype().
        vim.print(vim.fn.win_gettype())
<

    Attributes: ~
        Since: 0.4.0

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {win}  (`integer`) |window-ID|, or 0 for current window

    Return: ~
        (`vim.api.keyset.win_config`) Map defining the window configuration,
        see |nvim_open_win()|

nvim_win_set_config({win}, {config})                   *nvim_win_set_config()*
    Reconfigures the layout and properties of a window.
    ‚Ä¢ Updates only the given keys; unspecified (`nil`) keys will not be
      changed.
    ‚Ä¢ Can move a window to another tabpage.
    ‚Ä¢ Can transform a window to/from a float.
    ‚Ä¢ Keys `row` / `col` / `relative` must be specified together.
    ‚Ä¢ Cannot move the last window in a tabpage to a different one.

    Example: to convert a floating window to a "normal" split window, specify
    the `win` field: >lua
        vim.api.nvim_win_set_config(0, { split = 'above', win = vim.fn.win_getid(1), })
<

    Attributes: ~
        Since: 0.4.0

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {win}     (`integer`) |window-ID|, or 0 for current window
      ‚Ä¢ {config}  (`vim.api.keyset.win_config`) Map defining the window
                  configuration, see |nvim_open_win()|

    See also: ~
      ‚Ä¢ |nvim_open_win()|


