==============================================================================
Lua module: vim.lsp.client                                        *lsp-client*

*vim.lsp.Client*

    Fields: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {attached_buffers}      (`table<integer,string>`) Each buffer's last
                                used `languageId`.
      ‚Ä¢ {cancel_request}        (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, id: integer): boolean`)
                                See |Client:cancel_request()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {capabilities}          (`lsp.ClientCapabilities`) Capabilities
                                provided by the client (editor or tool), at
                                startup.
      ‚Ä¢ {commands}              (`table<string,fun(command: lsp.Command, ctx: table)>`)
                                Client commands. See |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {config}                (`vim.lsp.ClientConfig`) Copy of the config
                                passed to |vim.lsp.start()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {dynamic_capabilities}  (`lsp.DynamicCapabilities`) Capabilities
                                provided at runtime (after startup).
      ‚Ä¢ {exec_cmd}              (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, cmd: lsp.Command, context: {bufnr?: integer}?, handler: lsp.Handler?)`)
                                See |Client:exec_cmd()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {exit_timeout}          (`integer|boolean`, default: `false`) See
                                |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {flags}                 (`table`) Experimental client flags:
                                ‚Ä¢ {allow_incremental_sync}? (`boolean`,
                                  default: `true`) Allow using incremental
                                  sync for buffer edits
                                ‚Ä¢ {debounce_text_changes}? (`integer`,
                                  default: `150`) Debounce `didChange`
                                  notifications to the server by the given
                                  number in milliseconds.
      ‚Ä¢ {get_language_id}       (`fun(bufnr: integer, filetype: string): string`)
                                See |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {handlers}              (`table<string,lsp.Handler>`) See
                                |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {id}                    (`integer`) The id allocated to the client.
      ‚Ä¢ {initialized}           (`true?`)
      ‚Ä¢ {is_stopped}            (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client): boolean`) See
                                |Client:is_stopped()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {name}                  (`string`) See |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {notify}                (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, method: string, params: table?, bufnr: integer?): boolean`)
                                See |Client:notify()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {offset_encoding}       (`'utf-8'|'utf-16'|'utf-32'`) See
                                |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {on_attach}             (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, bufnr: integer)`)
                                See |Client:on_attach()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {progress}              (`vim.lsp.Client.Progress`) A ring buffer
                                (|vim.ringbuf()|) containing progress messages
                                sent by the server.
      ‚Ä¢ {request}               (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, method: string, params: table?, handler: lsp.Handler?, bufnr: integer?): boolean, integer?`)
                                See |Client:request()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {request_sync}          (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, method: string, params: table, timeout_ms: integer?, bufnr: integer?): {err: lsp.ResponseError?, result:any}?, string?`)
                                See |Client:request_sync()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {requests}              (`table<integer,{ type: string, bufnr: integer, method: vim.lsp.protocol.Method}?>`)
                                The current pending requests in flight to the
                                server. Entries are key-value pairs with the
                                key being the request id while the value is a
                                table with `type`, `bufnr`, and `method`
                                key-value pairs. `type` is either "pending"
                                for an active request, or "cancel" for a
                                cancel request. It will be "complete"
                                ephemerally while executing |LspRequest|
                                autocmds when replies are received from the
                                server.
      ‚Ä¢ {root_dir}              (`string?`) See |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {rpc}                   (`vim.lsp.rpc.Client`) RPC client object, for
                                low level interaction with the client. See
                                |vim.lsp.rpc.start()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {server_capabilities}   (`lsp.ServerCapabilities`) Response from the
                                server sent on `initialize` describing the
                                server's capabilities.
      ‚Ä¢ {server_info}           (`lsp.ServerInfo?`) Response from the server
                                sent on `initialize` describing server
                                information (e.g. version).
      ‚Ä¢ {settings}              (`lsp.LSPObject`) See |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
      ‚Ä¢ {stop}                  (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, force: integer|boolean?)`)
                                See |Client:stop()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {supports_method}       (`fun(self: vim.lsp.Client, method: string|string, bufnr: integer?): boolean`)
                                See |Client:supports_method()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {workspace_folders}     (`lsp.WorkspaceFolder[]?`) See
                                |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.

*vim.lsp.Client.Progress*
    Extends: |vim.Ringbuf|


    Fields: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {pending}  (`table<lsp.ProgressToken,lsp.LSPAny>`)

*vim.lsp.ClientConfig*

    Fields: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {before_init}?         (`fun(params: lsp.InitializeParams, config: vim.lsp.ClientConfig)`)
                               Callback which can modify parameters before
                               they are sent to the server. Invoked before LSP
                               "initialize" phase (after `cmd` is invoked),
                               where `params` is the parameters being sent to
                               the server and `config` is the config passed to
                               |vim.lsp.start()|.

                               Hint: use |vim.tbl_deep_extend()| to set nested
                               fields easily. >lua
                                   before_init = function(_, config)
                                     config.settings = vim.tbl_deep_extend('force',
                                       config.settings,
                                       { tailwindCSS = { experimental = { configFile = find_tailwind_global_css() } } }
                                     )
                                   end
<
      ‚Ä¢ {capabilities}?        (`lsp.ClientCapabilities`) Map overriding the
                               default capabilities defined by
                               |vim.lsp.protocol.make_client_capabilities()|,
                               passed to the language server on
                               initialization. Hint: use
                               make_client_capabilities() and modify its
                               result.
                               ‚Ä¢ Note: To send an empty dictionary use
                                 |vim.empty_dict()|, else it will be encoded
                                 as an array.
      ‚Ä¢ {cmd}                  (`string[]|fun(dispatchers: vim.lsp.rpc.Dispatchers, config: vim.lsp.ClientConfig): vim.lsp.rpc.Client`)
                               Command `string[]` that launches the language
                               server (treated as in |jobstart()|, must be
                               absolute or on `$PATH`, shell constructs like
                               "~" are not expanded), or function that creates
                               an RPC client (or an in-process |lsp-server|).
                               Function receives a `dispatchers` table and the
                               resolved `config`, and must return an object in
                               the form of |vim.lsp.rpc.Client|.

                               Example (list): >lua
                                   cmd = { 'flow', 'lsp' }
<

                               Example (function): >lua
                                   cmd = function(dispatchers, config)
                                     local cmd = vim.fn.executable('flow') == 1 and { 'flow', 'lsp' } or { 'npx', '--no-install', 'flow', 'lsp' }
                                     return vim.lsp.rpc.start(cmd, dispatchers)
                                   end
<
                               ‚Ä¢ See |vim.lsp.rpc.Client|.
                               ‚Ä¢ For TCP there is a builtin RPC client
                                 factory: |vim.lsp.rpc.connect()|
      ‚Ä¢ {cmd_cwd}?             (`string`) Directory where the `cmd` process is
                               launched when `cmd` is a string array. Defaults
                               to `root_dir` when available, then the current
                               working directory.
      ‚Ä¢ {cmd_env}?             (`table`) Environment variables passed to the
                               LSP process on spawn. Non-string values are
                               coerced to string. Example: >lua
                                   { PORT = 8080, HOST = '0.0.0.0' }
<
      ‚Ä¢ {commands}?            (`table<string,fun(command: lsp.Command, ctx: table)>`)
                               Map of client-defined commands overriding the
                               global |vim.lsp.commands|.
      ‚Ä¢ {detached}?            (`boolean`, default: `true`) Daemonize the
                               server process so that it runs in a separate
                               process group from Nvim. Nvim will shutdown the
                               process on exit, but if Nvim fails to exit
                               cleanly this could leave behind orphaned server
                               processes.
      ‚Ä¢ {exit_timeout}?        (`integer|boolean`, default: `false`) Decides
                               if/when to force-stop the server after sending
                               the "shutdown" request. See |Client:stop()|.
                               Note: when Nvim itself is exiting,
                               ‚Ä¢ `false`: Nvim will not force-stop LSP
                                 server(s).
                               ‚Ä¢ `true`: Nvim will force-stop LSP server(s)
                                 that did not comply with the "shutdown"
                                 request.
                               ‚Ä¢ `number`: Nvim will wait up to `exit_timeout`
                                 milliseconds before performing force-stop.
      ‚Ä¢ {flags}?               (`table`) Experimental client flags:
                               ‚Ä¢ {allow_incremental_sync}? (`boolean`,
                                 default: `true`) Allow using incremental sync
                                 for buffer edits
                               ‚Ä¢ {debounce_text_changes}? (`integer`, default:
                                 `150`) Debounce `didChange` notifications to
                                 the server by the given number in
                                 milliseconds.
      ‚Ä¢ {get_language_id}?     (`fun(bufnr: integer, filetype: string): string`)
                               Language ID as string. Defaults to the buffer
                               filetype.
      ‚Ä¢ {handlers}?            (`table<string,function>`) Map of LSP method
                               names to |lsp-handler|s.
      ‚Ä¢ {init_options}?        (`lsp.LSPObject`) Values to pass in the
                               initialization request as
                               `initializationOptions`. See `initialize` in
                               the LSP spec.
      ‚Ä¢ {name}?                (`string`, default: client-id) Name in logs and
                               user messages.
      ‚Ä¢ {offset_encoding}?     (`'utf-8'|'utf-16'|'utf-32'`) Called "position
                               encoding" in LSP spec. The encoding that the
                               LSP server expects, used for communication. Not
                               validated. Can be modified in `on_init` before
                               text is sent to the server.
      ‚Ä¢ {on_attach}?           (`elem_or_list<fun(client: vim.lsp.Client, bufnr: integer)>`)
                               Callback invoked when client attaches to a
                               buffer.
      ‚Ä¢ {on_error}?            (`fun(code: integer, err: string)`) Callback
                               invoked when the client operation throws an
                               error. `code` is a number describing the error.
                               Other arguments may be passed depending on the
                               error kind. See `vim.lsp.rpc.client_errors` for
                               possible errors. Use
                               `vim.lsp.rpc.client_errors[code]` to get
                               human-friendly name.
      ‚Ä¢ {on_exit}?             (`elem_or_list<fun(code: integer, signal: integer, client_id: integer)>`)
                               Callback invoked on client exit.
                               ‚Ä¢ code: exit code of the process
                               ‚Ä¢ signal: number describing the signal used to
                                 terminate (if any)
                               ‚Ä¢ client_id: client handle
      ‚Ä¢ {on_init}?             (`elem_or_list<fun(client: vim.lsp.Client, init_result: lsp.InitializeResult)>`)
                               Callback invoked after LSP "initialize", where
                               `result` is a table of `capabilities` and
                               anything else the server may send. For example,
                               clangd sends `init_result.offsetEncoding` if
                               `capabilities.offsetEncoding` was sent to it.
                               You can only modify the
                               `client.offset_encoding` here before any
                               notifications are sent.
      ‚Ä¢ {root_dir}?            (`string`) Directory where the LSP server will
                               base its workspaceFolders, rootUri, and
                               rootPath on initialization.
      ‚Ä¢ {settings}?            (`lsp.LSPObject`) Map of language
                               server-specific settings, decided by the
                               client. Sent to the LS if requested via
                               `workspace/configuration`. Keys are
                               case-sensitive.
      ‚Ä¢ {trace}?               (`'off'|'messages'|'verbose'`, default: "off")
                               Passed directly to the language server in the
                               initialize request. Invalid/empty values will
      ‚Ä¢ {workspace_folders}?   (`lsp.WorkspaceFolder[]`) List of workspace
                               folders passed to the language server. For
                               backwards compatibility rootUri and rootPath
                               are derived from the first workspace folder in
                               this list. Can be `null` if the client supports
                               workspace folders but none are configured. See
                               `workspaceFolders` in LSP spec.
      ‚Ä¢ {workspace_required}?  (`boolean`, default: `false`) Server requires a
                               workspace (no "single file" support). Note:
                               Without a workspace, cross-file features
                               (navigation, hover) may or may not work
                               depending on the language server, even if the
                               server doesn't require a workspace.


Client:cancel_request({id})                          *Client:cancel_request()*
    Cancels a request with a given request id.

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {id}  (`integer`) id of request to cancel

    Return: ~
        (`boolean`) status indicating if the notification was successful.

    See also: ~
      ‚Ä¢ |Client:notify()|

Client:exec_cmd({cmd}, {context}, {handler})               *Client:exec_cmd()*
    Execute a lsp command, either via client command function (if available)
    or via workspace/executeCommand (if supported by the server)

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {cmd}      (`lsp.Command`)
      ‚Ä¢ {context}  (`{bufnr?: integer}?`)
      ‚Ä¢ {handler}  (`lsp.Handler?`) only called if a server command

Client:is_stopped()                                      *Client:is_stopped()*
    Checks whether a client is stopped.

    Return: ~
        (`boolean`) true if client is stopped or in the process of being
        stopped; false otherwise

Client:notify({method}, {params}, {bufnr})                   *Client:notify()*
    Sends a notification to an LSP server.

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {method}  (`string`) LSP method name.
      ‚Ä¢ {params}  (`table?`) LSP request params.
      ‚Ä¢ {bufnr}   (`integer?`) Buffer associated with notification.

    Return: ~
        (`boolean`) status indicating if the notification was successful. If
        it is false, then the client has shutdown.

Client:on_attach({bufnr})                                 *Client:on_attach()*
    Runs the on_attach function from the client's config if it was defined.
    Useful for buffer-local setup.

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {bufnr}  (`integer`) Buffer number

                                                            *Client:request()*
Client:request({method}, {params}, {handler}, {bufnr})
    Sends a request to the server.

    This is a thin wrapper around {client.rpc.request} with some additional
    checks for capabilities and handler availability.

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {method}   (`string`) LSP method name.
      ‚Ä¢ {params}   (`table?`) LSP request params.
      ‚Ä¢ {handler}  (`lsp.Handler?`) Response |lsp-handler| for this method.
      ‚Ä¢ {bufnr}    (`integer?`) (default: 0) Buffer handle, or 0 for current.

    Return (multiple): ~
        (`boolean`) status indicates whether the request was successful. If it
        is `false`, then it will always be `false` (the client has shutdown).
        (`integer?`) request_id Can be used with |Client:cancel_request()|.
        `nil` is request failed.

    See also: ~
      ‚Ä¢ |vim.lsp.buf_request_all()|

                                                       *Client:request_sync()*
Client:request_sync({method}, {params}, {timeout_ms}, {bufnr})
    Sends a request to the server and synchronously waits for the response.

    This is a wrapper around |Client:request()|

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {method}      (`string`) LSP method name.
      ‚Ä¢ {params}      (`table`) LSP request params.
      ‚Ä¢ {timeout_ms}  (`integer?`) Maximum time in milliseconds to wait for a
                      result. Defaults to 1000
      ‚Ä¢ {bufnr}       (`integer?`) (default: 0) Buffer handle, or 0 for
                      current.

    Return (multiple): ~
        (`{err: lsp.ResponseError?, result:any}?`) `result` and `err` from the
        |lsp-handler|. `nil` is the request was unsuccessful
        (`string?`) err On timeout, cancel or error, where `err` is a string
        describing the failure reason.

    See also: ~
      ‚Ä¢ |vim.lsp.buf_request_sync()|

Client:stop({force})                                           *Client:stop()*
    Stops a client, optionally with force after a timeout.

    By default this sends a "shutdown" request to the server, escalating to
    force-stop if the server has not exited after `self.exit_timeout`
    milliseconds (unless `exit_timeout=false`). Calling stop() on a client
    that was previously requested to shutdown, will escalate to force-stop
    immediately, regardless of `force` (or `self.exit_timeout` if
    `force=nil`).

    Note: Forcing shutdown while a server is busy writing out project or index
    files can lead to file corruption.

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {force}  (`integer|boolean?`) (default: `self.exit_timeout`) Decides
                 whether to force-stop the server.
                 ‚Ä¢ `false`: Do not force-stop after "shutdown" request.
                 ‚Ä¢ `nil`: Defaults to `exit_timeout` from
                   |vim.lsp.ClientConfig|.
                 ‚Ä¢ `true`: Force-stop after "shutdown" request.
                 ‚Ä¢ number: Wait up to `force` milliseconds before force-stop.

Client:supports_method({method}, {bufnr})           *Client:supports_method()*
    Checks if a client supports a given method. Always returns true for
    unknown off-spec methods.

    Note: Some language server capabilities can be file specific.

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {method}  (`string|string`)
      ‚Ä¢ {bufnr}   (`integer?`)

    Return: ~
        (`boolean`)


