Lua module: vim.hl                                                    *vim.hl*

vim.hl.hl_op({opts})                                          *vim.hl.hl_op()*
    Highlights the affected text region during a |TextYankPost| or
    |TextPutPost| event.

    Add this to your `init.vim`: >vim
        autocmd TextYankPost * silent! lua vim.hl.hl_op {higroup='Visual', timeout=300}
        autocmd TextPutPost  * silent! lua vim.hl.hl_op {higroup='Visual', timeout=300}
<

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {opts}  (`table?`) Optional parameters
                ‚Ä¢ event (default: vim.v.event) Event structure.
                ‚Ä¢ higroup (default: "IncSearch") Highlight group for the text
                  region.
                ‚Ä¢ on_macro (default: false) Highlight during |macro|
                  execution.
                ‚Ä¢ on_visual (default: true) Highlight during |Visual| mode.
                ‚Ä¢ priority (default: |vim.hl.priorities|`.user`) Integer
                  priority.
                ‚Ä¢ timeout (default: 150) Time in ms before highlight is
                  cleared.

vim.hl.priorities                                          *vim.hl.priorities*
    Table with default priorities used for highlighting:
    ‚Ä¢ `syntax`: `50`, used for standard syntax highlighting
    ‚Ä¢ `treesitter`: `100`, used for treesitter-based highlighting
    ‚Ä¢ `semantic_tokens`: `125`, used for LSP semantic token highlighting
    ‚Ä¢ `diagnostics`: `150`, used for code analysis such as diagnostics
    ‚Ä¢ `user`: `200`, used for user-triggered highlights such as LSP document
      symbols or `hl_op` autocommands

                                                              *vim.hl.range()*
vim.hl.range({buf}, {ns}, {hlgroup}, {start}, {finish}, {opts})
    Apply highlight group to range of text.

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {buf}      (`integer`) Buffer number to apply highlighting to
      ‚Ä¢ {ns}       (`integer`) Namespace to add highlight to
      ‚Ä¢ {hlgroup}  (`integer|integer[]|string|string[]`) Highlight group used
                   for the text range. See the `hl_group` option in
                   |nvim_buf_set_extmark()|.
      ‚Ä¢ {start}    (`[integer,integer]|string`) Start of region as a (line,
                   column) tuple or string accepted by |getpos()|
      ‚Ä¢ {finish}   (`[integer,integer]|string`) End of region as a (line,
                   column) tuple or string accepted by |getpos()|
      ‚Ä¢ {opts}     (`table?`) A table with the following fields:
                   ‚Ä¢ {inclusive}? (`boolean`, default: `false`) Indicates
                     whether the range is end-inclusive
                   ‚Ä¢ {priority}? (`integer`, default:
                     `vim.hl.priorities.user`) Highlight priority
                   ‚Ä¢ {regtype}? (`string`, default: `'v'` i.e. charwise) Type
                     of range. See |getregtype()|
                   ‚Ä¢ {timeout}? (`integer`, default: -1 no timeout) Time in ms
                     before highlight is cleared

    Return (multiple): ~
        (`uv.uv_timer_t?`) range_timer A timer which manages how much time the
        highlight has left
        (`fun()?`) range_clear A function which allows clearing the highlight
        manually. nil is returned if timeout is not specified


==============================================================================
Lua module: vim.iter                                                *vim.iter*

*vim.iter()* is an interface for |iterable|s: it wraps a table or function
argument into an *Iter* object with methods (such as |Iter:filter()| and
|Iter:map()|) that transform the underlying source data. These methods can be
chained to create iterator "pipelines": the output of each pipeline stage is
input to the next stage. The first stage depends on the type passed to
`vim.iter()`:
‚Ä¢ Lists or arrays (|lua-list|) yield only the value of each element.
  ‚Ä¢ Holes (nil values) are allowed (but discarded).
  ‚Ä¢ Use pairs() to treat array/list tables as dicts (preserve holes and
    non-contiguous integer keys): `vim.iter(pairs(‚Ä¶))`.
  ‚Ä¢ Use |Iter:enumerate()| to also pass the index to the next stage.
    ‚Ä¢ Or initialize with ipairs(): `vim.iter(ipairs(‚Ä¶))`.
‚Ä¢ Non-list tables (|lua-dict|) yield both the key and value of each element.
‚Ä¢ Function |iterator|s yield all values returned by the underlying function.
‚Ä¢ Tables with a |__call()| metamethod are treated as function iterators.

The iterator pipeline terminates when the underlying |iterable| is exhausted
(for function iterators this means it returned nil).

Note: `vim.iter()` scans table input to decide if it is a list or a dict; to
avoid this cost you can wrap the table with an iterator e.g.
`vim.iter(ipairs({‚Ä¶}))`, but that precludes the use of |list-iterator|
operations such as |IterArray:rev()|).

Examples: >lua
    local it = vim.iter({ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 })
    it:map(function(v)
      return v * 3
    end)
    it:rev()
    it:skip(2)
    it:totable()
    -- { 9, 6, 3 }

    -- ipairs() is a function iterator which returns both the index (i) and the value (v)
    vim.iter(ipairs({ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 })):map(function(i, v)
      if i > 2 then return v end
    end):totable()
    -- { 3, 4, 5 }

    local it = vim.iter(vim.gsplit('1,2,3,4,5', ','))
    it:map(function(s) return tonumber(s) end)
    for i, d in it:enumerate() do
      print(string.format("Column %d is %d", i, d))
    end
    -- Column 1 is 1
    -- Column 2 is 2
    -- Column 3 is 3
    -- Column 4 is 4
    -- Column 5 is 5

    vim.iter({ a = 1, b = 2, c = 3, z = 26 }):any(function(k, v)
      return k == 'z'
    end)
    -- true

    local rb = vim.ringbuf(3)
    rb:push("a")
    rb:push("b")
    vim.iter(rb):totable()
    -- { "a", "b" }
<


Iter:all({pred})                                                  *Iter:all()*
    Returns true if all items in the iterator match the given predicate.

    Attributes: ~
        Since: 0.10.0

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {pred}  (`fun(v: V1, ...: V...): boolean`) Predicate function. Takes
                all values returned from the previous stage in the pipeline as
                arguments and returns true if the predicate matches.

Iter:any({pred})                                                  *Iter:any()*
    Returns true if any of the items in the iterator match the given
    predicate.

    Attributes: ~
        Since: 0.10.0

    Parameters: ~
      ‚Ä¢ {pred}  (`fun(v: V1, ...: V...): boolean`) Predicate function. Takes
